-- Q1: easy / L1 — Security criterion always required in SOC 2
(5, 'SOC Report Content and Structure', $EXPL$Which Trust Services Criterion must be included in every SOC 2 engagement?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Security", "Availability", "Processing Integrity", "Privacy"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Per the AICPA TSC framework, Security (also called the common criteria) is the mandatory foundation for all SOC 2 engagements. The remaining four criteria — Availability, Processing Integrity, Confidentiality, and Privacy — are included only when relevant to the services and user entity needs.
Wrong (B): Availability is optional, included when SLAs or uptime commitments are in scope.
Wrong (C): Processing Integrity is optional, included for transaction processing or financial calculation services.
Wrong (D): Privacy is optional, included only when the service organization processes personal information.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q2: medium / L2 — Section IV content makes SOC 2 restricted-use
(5, 'SOC Report Content and Structure', $EXPL$Ridgemont Data Services receives its first SOC 2 Type II report. The VP of Sales wants to post the full report on the company website to attract new clients. The compliance officer objects. The primary reason a SOC 2 report is restricted-use rather than general-use is that it contains:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Detailed control descriptions, tests performed, and test results", "The service organization's financial statements", "Pricing information for the service organization's offerings", "Personal information about the service organization's employees"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Per AT-C 205, a SOC 2 report is restricted-use because Section IV contains detailed descriptions of each control, the tests the service auditor performed, and the results — including any exceptions. This level of detail about the control environment is sensitive and should only be shared with parties who have sufficient understanding to interpret the findings.
Wrong (B): SOC 2 reports do not include financial statements; they address Trust Services Criteria.
Wrong (C): Pricing information is not part of any SOC report section.
Wrong (D): The report describes the system and controls, not personal employee information.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q3: medium / L3 — Control mapping: change management maps to multiple TSC
(5, 'SOC Report Content and Structure', $EXPL$During a SOC 2 Type II engagement at Hargrove Financial Systems, the auditor is mapping the change management approval process to applicable Trust Services Criteria. The auditor should map this control to:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Security criterion only", "Security and Availability only", "Security, Availability, and Processing Integrity", "All five Trust Services Criteria"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Per the AICPA TSC mapping guidance, a change management approval process addresses Security (preventing unauthorized changes), Availability (preventing outages from poorly tested changes), and Processing Integrity (ensuring changes do not corrupt processing logic). It does not inherently address Confidentiality or Privacy.
Wrong (A): Change management also prevents availability outages and processing corruption, not just unauthorized access.
Wrong (B): This omits Processing Integrity, which is also supported since unapproved changes could corrupt data processing.
Wrong (D): Change management does not directly address Confidentiality or Privacy criteria.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q4: medium / L3 — Bridge letter insufficiency when significant changes occur
(5, 'SOC Report Content and Structure', $EXPL$Calloway Manufacturing's external auditor is reviewing a service organization's SOC 1 Type II report covering January 1 through September 30. Calloway's fiscal year ends December 31. The service organization underwent a major system migration in November. For the three-month gap period, the auditor should:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Accept a bridge letter from service organization management as sufficient", "Issue a disclaimer of opinion on Calloway's financial statements", "Perform additional procedures such as inquiry and review of a subsequent SOC report", "Reduce the planned reliance on the service organization's controls to zero"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Per AU-C 402, when the gap period involves significant changes (such as a major system migration), a bridge letter alone is insufficient. The user entity auditor should perform additional procedures — including inquiry, observation, and review of any subsequent SOC report — to evaluate controls during the gap period.
Wrong (A): A bridge letter is generally sufficient only for short gaps with no significant changes, not when a major migration occurred.
Wrong (B): A disclaimer is an extreme measure not warranted solely by a gap period; the auditor should first perform additional procedures.
Wrong (D): Reducing reliance to zero is not the standard response; the auditor evaluates the impact through additional procedures rather than abandoning reliance entirely.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q5: medium / L2 — Four sections of a SOC 2 Type II report
(5, 'SOC Report Content and Structure', $EXPL$Pinnacle Cloud Services is preparing for its first SOC 2 Type II engagement. The engagement partner explains that the final report will contain four main sections. Which of the following correctly identifies all four sections?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Service auditor's opinion, management's assertion, system description, and description of controls/tests/results", "Management's assertion, system description, financial statements, and description of controls/tests/results", "Service auditor's opinion, management's assertion, risk assessment, and description of controls/tests/results", "Service auditor's opinion, system description, description of controls/tests/results, and internal audit report"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Per AICPA guidance on SOC 2 reporting (AT-C 205), a SOC 2 Type II report contains four sections: (I) the independent service auditor's report (opinion), (II) management's assertion, (III) the system description, and (IV) the description of controls, tests, and results.
Wrong (B): Financial statements are not part of a SOC 2 report; SOC 2 addresses Trust Services Criteria, not financial reporting.
Wrong (C): A risk assessment is not a standalone section; risk-related content is woven into the system description and control descriptions.
Wrong (D): An internal audit report is not a section of the SOC 2 report; the report is prepared by the external service auditor.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q6: medium / L2 — Service auditor opinion addresses three elements (EXCEPT)
(5, 'SOC Report Content and Structure', $EXPL$Ashford Technology Solutions has engaged a service auditor to perform a SOC 2 Type II examination. In Section I of the report, the service auditor's opinion addresses all of the following EXCEPT:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Whether the system description is fairly presented", "Whether the service organization's revenue recognition policies comply with GAAP", "Whether controls were suitably designed to meet applicable Trust Services Criteria", "Whether controls operated effectively throughout the specified period"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Per AT-C 205, the service auditor's opinion in a SOC 2 Type II report addresses three elements: (1) fair presentation of the system description, (2) suitability of control design, and (3) operating effectiveness of controls over the period. Revenue recognition and GAAP compliance are outside the scope of a SOC 2 engagement.
Wrong (A): Fair presentation of the system description is one of the three elements the opinion addresses.
Wrong (C): Suitability of control design is one of the three elements the opinion addresses.
Wrong (D): Operating effectiveness throughout the specified period is one of the three elements addressed in a Type II opinion.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q7: easy / L2 — AT-C 205 governs SOC 2 and SOC 3
(5, 'SOC Report Content and Structure', $EXPL$Glenview IT Consulting is reviewing the professional standards that govern SOC engagements. The engagement partner confirms that SOC 2 and SOC 3 reports are both performed under which AICPA standard?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["SSAE 18 / AT-C 320", "AT-C 105", "AT-C 205", "AU-C 315"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Per the AICPA, SOC 2 and SOC 3 engagements are performed under AT-C 205, which governs examination engagements using Trust Services Criteria. Both report types use the same underlying criteria and attestation standard.
Wrong (A): SSAE 18 / AT-C 320 governs SOC 1 engagements, which focus on controls relevant to user entities' financial reporting.
Wrong (B): AT-C 105 provides general concepts common to all attestation engagements but is not the specific standard for SOC 2/SOC 3.
Wrong (D): AU-C 315 addresses understanding the entity and its environment in a financial statement audit, not SOC engagements.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q8: medium / L3 — User entity auditor evaluating exceptions in Section IV
(5, 'SOC Report Content and Structure', $EXPL$Mercer Industries' external auditor is reviewing a service organization's SOC 1 Type II report. Section IV notes that 3 of 25 access control tests resulted in exceptions — unauthorized users retained access after role changes. The external auditor should:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Accept the report as reliable since over 88% of tests passed", "Return the report to the service organization and request that the exceptions be corrected", "Issue a qualified opinion on Mercer's financial statements due to the service organization's exceptions", "Evaluate whether the access control exceptions could affect Mercer's financial statement assertions"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Per AU-C 402, when exceptions are noted in a SOC report, the user entity auditor must evaluate whether those exceptions could affect the user entity's financial statements. The auditor considers the nature, cause, and potential impact of the exceptions on relevant assertions.
Wrong (A): A pass rate alone does not determine whether the exceptions are significant to the user entity's financial statements.
Wrong (B): The user entity auditor does not return the report for corrections; the report reflects historical testing results that cannot be retroactively changed.
Wrong (C): Exceptions in a SOC report do not automatically require qualification of the user entity's audit opinion; the auditor must first evaluate their impact.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q9: hard / L4 — Multi-criteria mapping evaluation for encryption at rest
(5, 'SOC Report Content and Structure', $EXPL$Kensington Financial's SOC 2 Type II engagement covers Security, Confidentiality, and Privacy. The service auditor is mapping data encryption at rest to applicable Trust Services Criteria. A junior team member argues that encryption at rest maps only to Confidentiality because its primary purpose is protecting data from unauthorized disclosure. The engagement manager should:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Agree and map encryption at rest to Confidentiality only", "Map encryption at rest to Security and Confidentiality only", "Map encryption at rest to Security, Confidentiality, and Privacy", "Map encryption at rest to all five Trust Services Criteria"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Per the AICPA TSC control mapping, data encryption at rest addresses Security (protection against unauthorized access), Confidentiality (protecting designated confidential information), and Privacy (protecting personal information). The junior team member's reasoning is too narrow — encryption simultaneously serves all three in-scope criteria.
Wrong (A): Encryption at rest also supports Security and Privacy, not just Confidentiality.
Wrong (B): This omits Privacy, which is also addressed when encryption protects personal information stored at rest.
Wrong (D): Encryption at rest does not directly support Availability or Processing Integrity; those criteria address uptime and processing accuracy respectively.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q10: easy / L1 — SOC 1 audience vs SOC 2 audience
(5, 'SOC Report Content and Structure', $EXPL$The primary intended audience for a SOC 1 report is:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The general public and prospective customers", "User entities and their financial statement auditors", "Government regulatory agencies exclusively", "The service organization's internal audit department"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Per SSAE 18 / AT-C 320, a SOC 1 report is designed specifically for user entities and their auditors who need to evaluate controls at a service organization relevant to the user entities' internal control over financial reporting (ICFR).
Wrong (A): The general public is the audience for a SOC 3 report, not SOC 1. SOC 1 reports are restricted-use.
Wrong (C): Regulatory agencies may receive the report as specified parties, but the primary intended audience is user entities and their auditors.
Wrong (D): Internal audit may use the report, but the primary audience is external — user entities and their financial statement auditors.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q11: easy / L1 — SOC 3 is Type II only
(5, 'SOC Report Content and Structure', $EXPL$Which statement about SOC 3 report types is correct?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["SOC 3 reports are available as both Type I and Type II", "SOC 3 reports are available as Type I only", "SOC 3 reports are available as Type II only", "SOC 3 reports do not use the Type I or Type II distinction"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Per AICPA guidance, SOC 3 reports are issued as Type II only. They are short-form, general-use reports based on a companion SOC 2 Type II examination. There is no SOC 3 Type I report.
Wrong (A): SOC 3 does not offer a Type I option; only SOC 1 and SOC 2 have both Type I and Type II.
Wrong (B): SOC 3 is Type II only, not Type I only.
Wrong (D): The Type distinction does apply — SOC 3 specifically requires a Type II examination as its basis.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q12: easy / L1 — CC9 Risk Mitigation scope
(5, 'SOC Report Content and Structure', $EXPL$In the SOC 2 common criteria framework, CC9 (Risk Mitigation) addresses which area?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Logical access controls and badge-based entry systems", "Internal and external communication about security policies", "Processes for managing identified risks including business continuity planning", "Detecting and responding to security events through SIEM monitoring"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Per the AICPA TSC framework, CC9 (Risk Mitigation) addresses processes for managing identified risks, including business continuity planning, insurance, and vendor management. These controls ensure that risks identified through the risk assessment process are appropriately addressed.
Wrong (A): Logical access controls and badge-based entry are addressed under CC6 (Logical and Physical Access Controls).
Wrong (B): Internal and external communication about security policies falls under CC2 (Communication and Information).
Wrong (D): Detecting and responding to security events through SIEM monitoring is addressed under CC7 (System Operations).$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q13: medium / L2 — CC6 logical and physical access controls
(5, 'SOC Report Content and Structure', $EXPL$Prescott Financial Services is undergoing a SOC 2 Type II examination. The service auditor is evaluating controls under CC6 of the common criteria. CC6 addresses which category of controls?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Logical and physical access controls", "Change management processes", "System operations and incident detection", "Communication and information policies"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Per the AICPA TSC framework, CC6 addresses logical and physical access controls — including multi-factor authentication, role-based access control, badge access, and network segmentation. These controls manage who and what can access the system and facilities.
Wrong (B): Change management processes are addressed under CC8, not CC6.
Wrong (C): System operations and incident detection are addressed under CC7, not CC6.
Wrong (D): Communication and information policies are addressed under CC2, not CC6.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q14: easy / L2 — SOC 2 restricted-use parties
(5, 'SOC Report Content and Structure', $EXPL$Hargrove Payroll Solutions has completed its SOC 2 Type II report. The compliance team asks who may receive the restricted-use report. Appropriate recipients include:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Only the service organization's management", "Management, user entities, user entity auditors, and other specified parties", "Any party that requests the report from the service organization", "Only regulatory agencies with enforcement authority"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Per AT-C 205, a SOC 2 report is restricted to management of the service organization, user entities, user entity auditors, and other specified parties who have sufficient understanding to interpret the detailed findings.
Wrong (A): The report is not limited to management alone; user entities and their auditors are also appropriate recipients.
Wrong (C): Unrestricted distribution describes a SOC 3 report, not SOC 2. SOC 2 is restricted-use.
Wrong (D): Regulatory agencies may receive the report as specified parties, but they are not the only permitted recipients.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q15: hard / L4 — Evaluating gap period with conflicting bridge letter
(5, 'SOC Report Content and Structure', $EXPL$Durant Healthcare's external auditor is planning year-end procedures for the December 31 fiscal year. The service organization's SOC 1 Type II report covers January 1 through June 30 — a six-month gap. The service organization provided a bridge letter stating no significant changes occurred, but the auditor learns the organization replaced its CEO and CFO in October. The MOST appropriate response is to:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Accept the bridge letter as sufficient since management represented no significant changes", "Disclaim an opinion on Durant's financial statements", "Perform additional procedures including inquiry about the leadership changes and their impact on the control environment", "Require the service organization to obtain a new SOC 1 report covering the full calendar year"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Per AU-C 402, when a gap period is long (six months) and there are indicators of potential changes — such as executive leadership turnover — the user entity auditor must exercise professional judgment and perform additional procedures. The bridge letter's assertion conflicts with known facts about C-suite changes, which can significantly affect the control environment (COSO CC1).
Wrong (A): The bridge letter's claim of no significant changes is contradicted by C-suite turnover, which directly affects tone at the top and the control environment.
Wrong (B): A disclaimer is premature; the auditor should first attempt additional procedures to evaluate the impact before concluding the evidence is insufficient.
Wrong (D): The auditor cannot require the service organization to obtain a new report; they can only perform their own additional procedures.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q16: medium / L3 — Processing Integrity points of focus
(5, 'SOC Report Content and Structure', $EXPL$Beacon Analytics is undergoing a SOC 2 Type II examination that includes the Processing Integrity criterion. The auditor needs to evaluate whether the system processes data completely and accurately. Which set of points of focus is MOST relevant to the Processing Integrity criterion?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["System recovery capabilities, backup procedures, and disaster recovery testing", "Input validation, processing accuracy, output completeness, and error handling", "Identification of confidential information, access restrictions, and secure disposal", "Notice, choice/consent, collection, and use/retention/disposal"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Per the AICPA TSC framework, the Processing Integrity criterion (PI1) focuses on input validation, processing accuracy, output completeness, and error handling and correction — ensuring that system processing is complete, valid, accurate, timely, and authorized.
Wrong (A): System recovery, backup, and disaster recovery are points of focus for the Availability criterion (A1).
Wrong (C): Identification of confidential information, access restrictions, and secure disposal are points of focus for the Confidentiality criterion (C1).
Wrong (D): Notice, choice/consent, collection, and use/retention/disposal are points of focus for the Privacy criterion (P1-P4).$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q17: hard / L4 — User entity auditor not required to reference service auditor
(5, 'SOC Report Content and Structure', $EXPL$Whitfield & Associates is auditing Meridian Corp.'s financial statements. Meridian relies heavily on a service organization whose SOC 1 Type II report had an unqualified opinion and no exceptions. After thorough evaluation, Whitfield places significant reliance on the SOC report. In issuing its audit report on Meridian's financial statements, Whitfield:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Must reference the service auditor and the SOC report in the audit opinion paragraph", "Is not required to reference the service auditor or the SOC report in the audit report", "Must include the SOC report as an appendix to the audit report", "Must issue a qualified opinion disclosing the reliance on the service organization"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Per AU-C 402, the user entity auditor is not required to reference the service auditor or the SOC report in their own audit report, even when placing significant reliance on it. The audit opinion covers the financial statements as a whole, and the user entity auditor takes full responsibility for the opinion.
Wrong (A): There is no requirement to reference the service auditor in the opinion paragraph; the user entity auditor assumes responsibility for the overall opinion.
Wrong (C): The SOC report is not appended to the audit report; it is a restricted-use document evaluated internally by the audit team.
Wrong (D): Reliance on a SOC report with an unqualified opinion does not require qualification of the audit opinion.$EXPL$, 'hard', 3),

-- Q18: hard / L3 — COSO components mapped to common criteria
(5, 'SOC Report Content and Structure', $EXPL$Waverly Insurance Group is preparing for a SOC 2 Type II examination. The engagement team explains that the Security common criteria are organized around the COSO Internal Control Framework. The auditor is evaluating the company's annual risk assessments and vulnerability scanning program. These activities most directly map to which COSO component within the common criteria?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["CC1 — Control Environment", "CC2 — Communication and Information", "CC3 — Risk Assessment", "CC5 — Control Activities"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Per the AICPA TSC framework, CC3 (Risk Assessment) addresses identification and assessment of risks to achieving security objectives. Annual risk assessments and vulnerability scanning are core examples of risk identification and assessment activities within this COSO component.
Wrong (A): CC1 (Control Environment) covers commitment to integrity, board oversight, and organizational structure — not risk assessment activities.
Wrong (B): CC2 (Communication and Information) covers internal and external communication about security policies, not risk identification processes.
Wrong (D): CC5 (Control Activities) covers policies and procedures that address identified risks, but the identification and assessment of those risks falls under CC3.$EXPL$, 'hard', 3),

-- Q19: medium / L3 — Privacy criteria P5 Access
(5, 'SOC Report Content and Structure', $EXPL$Sterling Health Data processes patient records for hospital clients. During a SOC 2 Type II engagement that includes Privacy, the auditor evaluates the eight privacy points of focus (P1 through P8). A user entity asks whether the Privacy criterion covers the company's obligation to allow individuals to access their own personal information. This requirement falls under:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["P1 — Notice", "P3 — Collection", "P5 — Access", "P7 — Quality"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Per the AICPA TSC framework, P5 (Access) addresses the requirement that individuals be given access to their personal information for review and update. This ensures data subjects can verify what personal data the organization holds about them.
Wrong (A): P1 (Notice) requires organizations to provide notice about their privacy practices, not to grant individuals access to their data.
Wrong (B): P3 (Collection) addresses how personal information is collected, not individual access rights to review stored data.
Wrong (D): P7 (Quality) addresses maintaining accurate and complete personal information, not the access mechanism itself.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q20: medium / L3 — Availability criterion: least relevant control
(5, 'SOC Report Content and Structure', $EXPL$Cascade Cloud Hosting includes the Availability criterion in its SOC 2 Type II engagement because it maintains SLA commitments of 99.9% uptime. The auditor evaluates Availability points of focus. Which control would LEAST support the Availability criterion?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Automated backup and recovery procedures", "Disaster recovery testing performed quarterly", "Data classification and encryption of files at rest", "Environmental protections including fire suppression and UPS systems"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Per the AICPA TSC framework, data classification and encryption at rest primarily support Security and Confidentiality, not Availability. Availability (A1) focuses on system recovery capabilities, backup procedures, disaster recovery testing, and environmental protections that maintain system uptime.
Wrong (A): Automated backup and recovery directly support system recovery, a core Availability point of focus.
Wrong (B): Disaster recovery testing validates the organization's ability to restore services, directly supporting Availability.
Wrong (D): Environmental protections such as fire suppression and UPS systems protect physical infrastructure availability.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q21: medium / L3 — MFA maps to security, confidentiality, and privacy
(5, 'SOC Report Content and Structure', $EXPL$Oakridge Financial's SOC 2 Type II engagement covers Security, Availability, Confidentiality, and Privacy. The auditor is determining which Trust Services Criteria are satisfied by the firm's multi-factor authentication control. MFA should be mapped to:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Security, Confidentiality, and Privacy", "Security and Availability", "All four in-scope criteria", "Security only"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Per the AICPA TSC control mapping, multi-factor authentication addresses Security (protecting against unauthorized access), Confidentiality (restricting access to confidential information), and Privacy (restricting access to personal information). MFA does not directly support Availability or Processing Integrity.
Wrong (B): MFA does not support Availability, which focuses on system uptime and recovery capabilities rather than access restriction.
Wrong (C): MFA does not address Availability, so it does not satisfy all four in-scope criteria.
Wrong (D): MFA supports Confidentiality and Privacy in addition to Security by restricting who can access protected data.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q22: easy / L2 — System description five components
(5, 'SOC Report Content and Structure', $EXPL$Thornton Data Services is drafting the system description (Section III) for its SOC 2 Type II report. The engagement team explains that the description must identify the five components of the system. These components are:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Hardware, operating systems, databases, firewalls, and backups", "Infrastructure, software, people, procedures, and data", "Risk assessment, control activities, monitoring, information, and communication", "Policies, standards, guidelines, procedures, and baselines"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Per AICPA SOC 2 guidance, the system description must identify five components: infrastructure, software, people, procedures, and data. These define the boundaries and scope of the system being examined.
Wrong (A): While these may be elements of infrastructure and software, they do not represent the five system components defined by AICPA guidance.
Wrong (C): These are the five components of the COSO internal control framework, not the system description components.
Wrong (D): These represent a hierarchy of governance documents, not the SOC 2 system description components.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q23: medium / L2 — SOC 1 governed by SSAE 18 / AT-C 320
(5, 'SOC Report Content and Structure', $EXPL$A user entity auditor at Fletcher & Associates needs to understand the professional standard that governs a service organization's SOC 1 Type II report. SOC 1 engagements are performed under:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["AT-C 205", "AU-C 402", "SSAE 18 / AT-C 320", "PCAOB AS 2601"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Per the AICPA, SOC 1 engagements are performed under SSAE 18 (Statement on Standards for Attestation Engagements No. 18), specifically AT-C Section 320, which addresses reporting on controls at a service organization relevant to user entities' internal control over financial reporting.
Wrong (A): AT-C 205 governs SOC 2 and SOC 3 engagements, not SOC 1.
Wrong (B): AU-C 402 provides guidance for user entity auditors on how to consider and use a service organization's SOC report, not the standard for performing the SOC 1 engagement itself.
Wrong (D): PCAOB AS 2601 is the PCAOB standard for service organizations applicable to issuer audits, not the AICPA standard governing SOC 1 engagements.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q24: hard / L4 — Evaluating service auditor independence
(5, 'SOC Report Content and Structure', $EXPL$Whitaker Industries' external auditor receives a SOC 1 Type II report for a payroll service organization. Before placing reliance, the auditor learns that the service auditor's firm also provides IT consulting services to the same service organization, including implementing the access control system tested in the report. The user entity auditor's MOST appropriate action is to:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Accept the report since the service auditor is responsible for its own independence", "Evaluate whether the service auditor's independence may be impaired and consider the effect on the reliability of the SOC report", "Refuse to use the SOC report and perform direct testing at the service organization", "Issue a qualified audit opinion on Whitaker's financial statements"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Per AU-C 402, before placing reliance on a SOC report, the user entity auditor must evaluate the competence and independence of the service auditor. When the same firm implemented the access controls it then tested, this creates a self-review threat to independence that the user entity auditor must assess for its potential impact on report reliability.
Wrong (A): The user entity auditor has an independent obligation to evaluate the service auditor's competence and independence, not simply defer to the service auditor's own assessment.
Wrong (C): Refusing the report entirely is premature; the auditor should first evaluate whether the independence concern materially affects reliability before deciding on alternative procedures.
Wrong (D): A qualified opinion is not warranted solely because of a potential independence concern with the service auditor; the auditor must first evaluate the situation.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q25: easy / L1 — Management assertion includes operating effectiveness in Type II
(5, 'SOC Report Content and Structure', $EXPL$In a SOC 2 Type II report, management's written assertion (Section II) includes a claim that controls operated effectively throughout the specified period AND:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The system description is fairly presented and controls were suitably designed", "The service organization complied with all applicable laws and regulations", "The service organization exceeded all industry security benchmarks", "The controls were approved by the service organization's board of directors"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Per AT-C 205, in a Type II engagement, management's assertion covers three claims: (1) the system description is fairly presented, (2) controls were suitably designed, and (3) controls operated effectively throughout the specified period. Fair presentation and suitable design accompany the operating effectiveness assertion.
Wrong (B): Legal and regulatory compliance is outside the scope of management's assertion in a SOC 2 report.
Wrong (C): The assertion addresses suitability of design and operating effectiveness, not benchmarking against industry standards.
Wrong (D): Board approval is not a required element of the management assertion.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q26: hard / L4 — SOC report type selection for dual needs (ICFR + public)
(5, 'SOC Report Content and Structure', $EXPL$Bradshaw Holdings operates a data processing center that handles financial transactions for clients whose auditors need to evaluate controls over financial reporting. Additionally, Bradshaw's marketing team wants a report they can share publicly to demonstrate security posture. The compliance officer should recommend:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["A single SOC 2 Type II report to satisfy both needs", "A SOC 1 Type II report for the auditors and a SOC 3 report for public distribution", "A SOC 3 report that can serve both the auditors and the public", "A SOC 2 Type I report for auditors and a separate SOC 2 Type II report for marketing"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Per the AICPA SOC framework, a SOC 1 Type II report (AT-C 320) is designed specifically for user entities and their auditors to evaluate controls over financial reporting — matching the auditors' ICFR need. A SOC 3 report (AT-C 205) is general-use and can be publicly distributed for marketing purposes. Together they address both requirements.
Wrong (A): A SOC 2 report is restricted-use and cannot be shared publicly; it also focuses on Trust Services Criteria rather than controls over financial reporting.
Wrong (C): A SOC 3 is a summary report without detailed control descriptions; auditors evaluating ICFR need a SOC 1 report with full test results.
Wrong (D): Neither a Type I nor Type II SOC 2 report addresses ICFR, and SOC 2 reports cannot be used for public marketing due to restricted distribution.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q27: medium / L2 — CC7 system operations
(5, 'SOC Report Content and Structure', $EXPL$During a SOC 2 Type II examination of Ridgemont Cloud Services, the auditor is testing controls classified under CC7 of the common criteria. The auditor would MOST likely evaluate:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Organizational commitment to integrity and ethical values", "Security event detection and response procedures including SIEM monitoring", "Change approval processes and testing procedures", "Board oversight structure and reporting lines"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Per the AICPA TSC framework, CC7 (System Operations) addresses detecting and responding to security events, including SIEM monitoring, intrusion detection/prevention systems, and vulnerability management. These controls ensure the organization can identify and respond to operational security incidents.
Wrong (A): Organizational commitment to integrity and ethical values falls under CC1 (Control Environment).
Wrong (C): Change approval processes and testing are addressed under CC8 (Change Management), not CC7.
Wrong (D): Board oversight structure and reporting lines fall under CC1 (Control Environment).$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q28: medium / L2 — Confidentiality criterion EXCEPT
(5, 'SOC Report Content and Structure', $EXPL$Weston Legal Technology is including the Confidentiality criterion in its SOC 2 Type II engagement because it handles clients' trade secrets and intellectual property. The auditor evaluating Confidentiality (C1) would focus on all of the following EXCEPT:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Identification of information designated as confidential", "Access restrictions limiting who can view confidential data", "System uptime commitments and SLA monitoring", "Secure disposal procedures for confidential information"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Per the AICPA TSC framework, system uptime commitments and SLA monitoring are points of focus for the Availability criterion (A1), not Confidentiality (C1). Confidentiality focuses on identifying confidential information, restricting access, encrypting data, and ensuring secure disposal.
Wrong (A): Identifying information designated as confidential is a core C1 point of focus.
Wrong (B): Access restrictions limiting who can view confidential data are central to C1.
Wrong (D): Secure disposal procedures for confidential information are a key C1 point of focus.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q29: easy / L1 — SOC 2 Type II typical examination period
(5, 'SOC Report Content and Structure', $EXPL$A SOC 2 Type II report typically covers an examination period of:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["1 to 3 months", "6 to 12 months", "2 to 3 years", "5 years or more"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Per AICPA SOC 2 guidance, a SOC 2 Type II report typically covers a period of 6 to 12 months. This provides sufficient time for the service auditor to evaluate whether controls operated effectively on an ongoing basis throughout the specified period.
Wrong (A): A 1 to 3 month period is too short to demonstrate sustained operating effectiveness of controls.
Wrong (C): A 2 to 3 year period exceeds the standard examination window; SOC reports are typically renewed annually.
Wrong (D): SOC reports do not span 5 or more years; they are issued annually to provide current assurance.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q30: medium / L3 — Incident response plan maps to multiple TSC
(5, 'SOC Report Content and Structure', $EXPL$Crestview Data Centers maintains a comprehensive incident response plan that covers detection, containment, eradication, and recovery from security incidents. During a SOC 2 Type II engagement covering Security, Availability, Confidentiality, and Privacy, the auditor is mapping this control. The incident response plan should be mapped to:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Security only, since incident response is fundamentally a security function", "Security, Availability, Confidentiality, and Privacy", "Security and Availability only", "Security, Confidentiality, and Privacy only"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Per the AICPA TSC control mapping, an incident response plan addresses Security (responding to security events), Availability (restoring system access after incidents), Confidentiality (containing breaches of confidential data), and Privacy (addressing unauthorized disclosure of personal information). It is one of the broadest-mapping controls across Trust Services Criteria.
Wrong (A): Incident response extends well beyond security alone — it also addresses availability restoration and data breach containment.
Wrong (C): This omits Confidentiality and Privacy, which are addressed when incidents involve exposure of confidential or personal data.
Wrong (D): This omits Availability, which is directly supported by the recovery phase of incident response.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3)
;

